# Sorting — `Comparable` & `Comparator`

## `Comparable` — the type's natural order

```java
public record Money(long cents) implements Comparable<Money> {
    public int compareTo(Money o) { return Long.compare(cents, o.cents); }
}
Collections.sort(payments);
```

**Rule:** compare, don't subtract — `a - b` can overflow. Use `Integer.compare`.

## `Comparator` — orderings on demand

```java
people.sort(Comparator.comparing(Person::lastName)
                      .thenComparing(Person::firstName));
people.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(Person::age).reversed());
people.sort(comparing(Person::nick, nullsLast(naturalOrder())));
```

`comparing` · `thenComparing` (tie-break) · `reversed` · `nullsFirst/Last`. Use `comparingInt` for primitives (no boxing).

## Where it happens

- `list.sort(cmp)` — in place, stable, O(n log n)
- `new TreeSet<>(cmp)` — stays sorted · streams `.sorted(cmp)`

**Choose:** one canonical order → `Comparable`; many orderings → `Comparator`.
